// http test root — `cpc test` discovers the `#[test]` fns in the modules this
// imports, and the imports are the point of the file.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. Without it, `cpc test` falls back to the package's source
// entry, which is `http.cplus` — and `http.cplus` deliberately does NOT import
// the transport. The two halves meet at a stable symbol
// (`http_transport_perform_v1`) rather than through an import, because the
// transport must import `http` for Request and Response and a cycle back is
// rejected outright (E0404). So nothing in the package's own import graph
// reaches the transport, and a test driver built from that graph linked against
// a symbol nothing defined.
//
// A CONSUMER NEVER HITS THAT, and it is worth knowing why the two differ: a
// package's archive is built from a SYNTHESIZED entry importing every module in
// `src/` (that is what `prebuild` means), so the transport is in the archive an
// application links whether or not anything imported it. The test driver is the
// one build that uses the real entry.
//
// The tests themselves stay next to what they test: the portable half's in
// `http.cplus`, the Foundation half's in `transport.cplus`.

import "./http" as http;
import "./transport" as transport;
